  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nScattered rain showers continue to spread northwards into\ncentral Minnesota & western Wisconsin this afternoon. These\nshowers will begin to wane as the low level warm advection\ndiminishes through the day, expect rainfall amounts of \n0.1-0.25\" wherever showers occur. We looks to be dry, but damp, \nfor trick or treaters this evening with areas of drizzle then \ndeveloping late tonight & through the overnight hours. Gloomy \nskies look to stick around through Sunday with little change in\nthe airmass expected to scatter out the clouds, however a few \npeeks of sunshine looks possible later in the afternoon. \nTemperatures will warm into the low to mid 60s despite the \nclouds, as southerly winds & warm advection strengthen through \nthe day.\n\nThe main weather of note this week comes Monday into Tuesday\nwhen a deep trough digs over the intermountain west & spins up a\nsurface low tracking from the central plains into the upper\nmidwest. Legacy physical models vary somewhat on the track of\nthe surface low, but the AI GFS & ECMWF solutions have remained\nconsistent with the low tracking directly over our area from \neastern SD into Lake Superior. This would place the favored \nlocation for the heaviest rain across the eastern Dakotas into \nwest-central Minnesota, a solution well-supported by the AI &\nphysical-based ECMWF ensembles. This will be a very wet system \nfor early November with near-record precipitable, helping to \ngenerate a widespread soaking rain event for the region from \nMonday morning through at least Tuesday morning. Widespread \nrainfall amounts of 1-2\" are expected across the entirety of \nMinnesota & western Wisconsin, with the best chances for 2-3\" \noccurring across west-central Minnesota. Most of this rain will\nfall within a few hours Monday morning & afternoon, but we're \nnot expecting convective rainfall rates capable of any flash \nflooding. Heading into Tuesday, the system does slow down & \nlinger over the Upper Midwest although models remain spread on \nhow long the light rain in the wrap-around region sticks \naround. AI solutions we could have lingering showers all the way\ninto Thursday, although additional rainfall amounts late \nTuesday into Thursday will only be on the order of 0.25-0.5\". \nWould be a great snowstorm in another November, but we'll settle\nfor continuing to chip away at our precipitation deficit for \n2026.\n\nTemperatures will remain fairly steady in the upper 50s to low\n60s through the heaviest rain Monday afternoon, then we'll see\nseveral days of classic November weather arrive as a colder\nairmass settles over the region behind the system. Highs in the\nmid to upper 40s will prevail into the weekend, along with\nwarmer lows in the upper 30s to low 40s as clouds looks to stick\naround the the week. Conditions look dry well into next week once\nwe clear out the lingering showers Wednesday-Thursday.",
